1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,338
Put two lists side by side.

2
00:00:02,338 --> 00:00:06,963
You can ask for everything on them,
for only what they share, or for what

3
00:00:06,963 --> 00:00:08,302
only one list has.

4
00:00:08,702 --> 00:00:09,912
Same two lists.

5
00:00:09,912 --> 00:00:11,112
Three questions.

6
00:00:11,112 --> 00:00:12,784
Three different answers.

7
00:00:13,234 --> 00:00:17,090
Diya hit this today, planning a
fruit salad with her friend Meera.

8
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:20,439
Diya likes mango, apple, guava and banana.

9
00:00:20,439 --> 00:00:22,973
Meera likes apple, mango and grapes.

10
00:00:23,373 --> 00:00:24,580
Buy every fruit?

11
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:26,600
Only the ones they both like?

12
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:28,572
Or the ones only Diya likes?

13
00:00:29,072 --> 00:00:31,338
Each question is a set operation.

14
00:00:31,338 --> 00:00:35,389
Today we build all three: union,
intersection and difference.

15
00:00:36,039 --> 00:00:39,132
Call Diya's fruits set A,
and Meera's fruits set B.

16
00:00:39,482 --> 00:00:44,380
The union of A and B is the set of
all elements that are in A, or in B,

17
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:45,351
or in both.

18
00:00:45,751 --> 00:00:47,716
Its symbol looks like a cup.

19
00:00:47,716 --> 00:00:49,543
We read it as A union B.

20
00:00:49,943 --> 00:00:51,060
Watch it build.

21
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:53,302
First, every fruit of A goes in.

22
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:55,374
Mango, apple, guava, banana.

23
00:00:55,774 --> 00:00:56,674
Now B.

24
00:00:56,494 --> 00:00:58,308
Apple is already inside.

25
00:00:58,308 --> 00:01:00,123
Mango is already inside.

26
00:01:00,123 --> 00:01:03,440
In a set, each element
is written only once.

27
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:05,245
Only grapes is new.

28
00:01:05,245 --> 00:01:08,065
So A union B has five fruits, not seven.

29
00:01:08,715 --> 00:01:14,099
In set builder form, A union B is the
set of all x, such that x is in A,

30
00:01:14,099 --> 00:01:15,369
or x is in B.

31
00:01:16,119 --> 00:01:17,446
Now Meera's question.

32
00:01:17,446 --> 00:01:19,933
Only the fruits that both of them like.

33
00:01:20,283 --> 00:01:24,708
The intersection of A and B is the
set of elements that belong to both

34
00:01:24,708 --> 00:01:25,608
A and B.

35
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:28,298
Its symbol is the cup turned upside down.

36
00:01:28,698 --> 00:01:29,696
Run the test.

37
00:01:29,696 --> 00:01:32,767
Take each fruit of A
and ask: is it in B too?

38
00:01:33,167 --> 00:01:34,067
Mango?

39
00:01:33,675 --> 00:01:34,575
Yes.

40
00:01:34,101 --> 00:01:35,001
Apple?

41
00:01:34,609 --> 00:01:35,509
Yes.

42
00:01:35,035 --> 00:01:35,935
Guava?

43
00:01:35,543 --> 00:01:36,443
No.

44
00:01:35,927 --> 00:01:36,827
Banana?

45
00:01:36,477 --> 00:01:37,377
No.

46
00:01:37,261 --> 00:01:40,035
So A intersection B is mango and apple.

47
00:01:40,035 --> 00:01:43,224
It lives exactly where
the two circles overlap.

48
00:01:43,874 --> 00:01:48,989
In set builder form, it is the set
of all x, such that x is in A, and x

49
00:01:48,989 --> 00:01:49,889
is in B.

50
00:01:50,582 --> 00:01:52,957
What if two sets share nothing at all?

51
00:01:52,957 --> 00:01:55,514
Take the odd numbers and the even numbers.

52
00:01:55,914 --> 00:01:57,663
Pull their circles apart.

53
00:01:57,663 --> 00:02:01,555
No number is both odd and
even, so the overlap is empty.

54
00:02:01,955 --> 00:02:06,183
When the intersection is the empty
set, we call the two sets disjoint.

55
00:02:06,833 --> 00:02:08,458
Now Diya's last question.

56
00:02:08,458 --> 00:02:10,582
Which fruits does only she like?

57
00:02:10,932 --> 00:02:15,226
The difference A minus B is the set
of elements that are in A, but not

58
00:02:15,226 --> 00:02:16,126
in B.

59
00:02:16,203 --> 00:02:17,428
Start with A.

60
00:02:17,428 --> 00:02:20,354
Remove every fruit that is also in B.

61
00:02:20,354 --> 00:02:21,397
Mango goes.

62
00:02:21,397 --> 00:02:22,440
Apple goes.

63
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:24,735
Guava and banana are left.

64
00:02:24,735 --> 00:02:28,063
That is A minus B, the
fruits only Diya likes.

65
00:02:28,713 --> 00:02:33,849
In set builder form, it is the set
of all x, such that x is in A, and x

66
00:02:33,849 --> 00:02:34,960
is not in B.

67
00:02:35,710 --> 00:02:36,914
Side by side.

68
00:02:36,914 --> 00:02:39,512
A minus B keeps what only A has.

69
00:02:39,512 --> 00:02:40,939
Guava and banana.

70
00:02:41,339 --> 00:02:44,018
B minus A keeps what only B has.

71
00:02:44,018 --> 00:02:45,133
Just grapes.

72
00:02:45,733 --> 00:02:48,834
So, is A minus B the same as B minus A?

73
00:02:48,834 --> 00:02:50,175
Pause and decide.

74
00:02:53,375 --> 00:02:54,275
No!

75
00:02:53,830 --> 00:02:55,146
The order matters.

76
00:02:55,146 --> 00:03:00,202
A minus B and B minus A are different
sets, and they never share an element.

77
00:03:00,852 --> 00:03:01,946
Remember this.

78
00:03:01,946 --> 00:03:03,367
Or joins the sets.

79
00:03:03,367 --> 00:03:05,258
And keeps what they share.

80
00:03:05,258 --> 00:03:06,567
Minus takes away.

81
00:03:07,267 --> 00:03:09,099
Look at circle A once more.

82
00:03:09,099 --> 00:03:10,837
It is made of two pieces.

83
00:03:11,237 --> 00:03:14,034
The part outside B, which is A minus B.

84
00:03:14,034 --> 00:03:16,969
And the overlap, which
is A intersection B.

85
00:03:17,369 --> 00:03:20,081
Join the two pieces, and
you get all of A back.

86
00:03:20,681 --> 00:03:21,581
Quick one.

87
00:03:21,535 --> 00:03:24,636
If A and B are disjoint,
what is A minus B?

88
00:03:24,636 --> 00:03:25,843
Pause and think.

89
00:03:29,043 --> 00:03:30,225
It is A itself!

90
00:03:30,225 --> 00:03:33,159
There is no overlap, so
nothing gets removed.

91
00:03:33,809 --> 00:03:35,545
Now, one worked example.

92
00:03:35,895 --> 00:03:38,143
A is the set of factors of twelve.

93
00:03:38,143 --> 00:03:40,483
B is the set of factors of eighteen.

94
00:03:40,483 --> 00:03:43,888
Find the union, the intersection,
and both differences.

95
00:03:44,388 --> 00:03:45,288
Step one.

96
00:03:45,128 --> 00:03:48,143
A is one, two, three,
four, six and twelve.

97
00:03:48,893 --> 00:03:49,793
Step two.

98
00:03:49,668 --> 00:03:52,919
B is one, two, three,
six, nine and eighteen.

99
00:03:53,669 --> 00:03:54,572
Step three.

100
00:03:54,572 --> 00:03:57,852
The common factors are
one, two, three and six.

101
00:03:57,852 --> 00:03:59,619
That is A intersection B.

102
00:04:00,369 --> 00:04:01,269
Step four.

103
00:04:01,250 --> 00:04:03,792
Join them, writing each factor once.

104
00:04:03,792 --> 00:04:05,879
A union B has eight elements.

105
00:04:06,629 --> 00:04:07,564
Step five.

106
00:04:07,564 --> 00:04:09,844
A minus B is four and twelve.

107
00:04:09,844 --> 00:04:12,231
B minus A is nine and eighteen.

108
00:04:12,981 --> 00:04:13,881
And look.

109
00:04:13,745 --> 00:04:18,049
The biggest number in the intersection,
six, is the highest common factor

110
00:04:18,049 --> 00:04:19,577
of twelve and eighteen.

111
00:04:20,227 --> 00:04:21,692
Now, the exam trap.

112
00:04:22,042 --> 00:04:23,462
A is five and seven.

113
00:04:23,462 --> 00:04:24,836
B is two and three.

114
00:04:24,836 --> 00:04:29,315
Many students subtract the numbers,
and write A minus B as three and four.

115
00:04:29,715 --> 00:04:32,215
But set difference never does arithmetic.

116
00:04:32,215 --> 00:04:34,235
It only removes shared elements.

117
00:04:34,235 --> 00:04:38,172
A and B share nothing, so A
minus B is still five and seven.

118
00:04:38,822 --> 00:04:40,407
Where is this asked?

119
00:04:40,407 --> 00:04:44,568
In PUC board exams, as direct
questions on two given sets.

120
00:04:44,568 --> 00:04:49,707
And in JEE Main, where union and
intersection are mixed with number rules.

121
00:04:50,307 --> 00:04:51,564
Exam level now.

122
00:04:51,564 --> 00:04:54,205
How many elements are in A minus B?

123
00:04:54,205 --> 00:04:55,515
Pause and solve.

124
00:05:00,915 --> 00:05:02,329
The answer is B.

125
00:05:02,329 --> 00:05:03,448
Twenty seven.

126
00:05:03,948 --> 00:05:06,733
A is the multiples of
three up to one hundred.

127
00:05:06,733 --> 00:05:10,146
That is three up to ninety
nine, so thirty three numbers.

128
00:05:10,646 --> 00:05:13,598
We must remove the ones
that are also in B.

129
00:05:13,598 --> 00:05:18,499
Those are multiples of both three and
five, which means multiples of fifteen.

130
00:05:18,999 --> 00:05:23,041
Fifteen, thirty, forty five,
sixty, seventy five and ninety.

131
00:05:23,041 --> 00:05:24,478
That is six numbers.

132
00:05:24,478 --> 00:05:27,198
Thirty three minus six
leaves twenty seven.

133
00:05:27,798 --> 00:05:29,258
Here is your recap.

134
00:05:29,258 --> 00:05:30,759
Screenshot this card.

135
00:05:33,959 --> 00:05:36,795
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

136
00:05:37,195 --> 00:05:38,095
One.

137
00:05:37,628 --> 00:05:40,908
What is the union of the
sets one, two and two, three?

138
00:05:41,808 --> 00:05:42,708
Two.

139
00:05:42,317 --> 00:05:44,620
When are two sets called disjoint?

140
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,420
Three.

141
00:05:46,069 --> 00:05:50,502
If A is one, two, three and B is
three and four, what is B minus A?

142
00:05:51,602 --> 00:05:55,593
Next time: everyone in class who
did not ride a bicycle today.

143
00:05:55,593 --> 00:05:57,421
That set has its own name.

144
00:05:57,421 --> 00:05:59,288
It is called the complement.
